<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qarmatians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qarmatian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Qarmatians were a radical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> movement based mainly in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> from the late ninth century. They raided widely and famously seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-stone">Black Stone</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 930. Although related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in origin, they attacked Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in its early years, a threat the Fatimid Caliphate repelled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qarmatians.jpg" alt="Qarmatians" width="320" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dpstoksy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qarmatian_Republic.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Qarmatians were an Isma'ili Shia militant movement led by an Arabized dynasty of Persian descent that claimed Alid descent. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> various Shiite groups organised in secret opposition. After the death of Muhammad ibn Isma'il, most of the proto-Isma'ili Mubarakiyyah denied that he had died and recognised him as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>. Their missionary movement had great success in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, where al-Husayn al-Ahwazi converted Hamdan in 874; he took the name Qarmat, and with his brother-in-law Abdan he prepared southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> for the coming of the Mahdi. A change of leadership at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamiyah">Salamiyah</a> in 899 split the movement, because the new leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-al-mahdi-billah">Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah</a>, taught that Muhammad had died and that he himself was his descendant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Their main stronghold was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, then covering much of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj-rebellion">Zanj Rebellion</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> had disrupted Abbasid power, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-al-jannabi">Abu Sa'id al-Jannabi</a>, an Arabized Persian from Jannaba in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, took Hajr and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Hasa</a> in 899 and made al-Hasa his capital. The Qarmatians rejected the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> as an illicit innovation and raided the pilgrim routes, and in 906 they massacred 20,000 pilgrims returning from Mecca. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-tahir-al-jannabi">Abu Tahir al-Jannabi</a> they came close to capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 927 and sacked Mecca in 930, desecrating the Zamzam Well with the corpses of pilgrims and carrying off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-stone">Black Stone</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> ransomed in 952.</p><p>For much of the tenth century they were the leading force in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, collecting tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, whose claim to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> they rejected. In 931 Abu Tahir handed power to a young Persian, Abu'l-Fadl al-Isfahani, whom the Qarmatians believed to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, but he executed nobles and insulted the prophets and was put to death after 80 days, which badly damaged the movement's credibility. After a defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> in 976 the Qarmatians became a local power, and by the middle of the eleventh century the Uyunid dynasty had replaced them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Their land had vast fruit and grain estates worked, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-khusraw">Nasir Khusraw</a>, who visited Hasa in 1051, by some thirty thousand Ethiopian slaves. He wrote that the people of Hasa paid no taxes and could borrow without interest. Some scholars have called the state proto-socialist, Perry Anderson called it an egalitarian slave-owning republic, and Azmi Bishara has called such claims an anachronistic projection of modern ideologies. In southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> the Qarmatians were known as the Greengrocers because Abu Hatim al-Zutti forbade animal slaughter and certain vegetables.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qarmatians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Qarmatians</b>, an Ismaili movement based in eastern Arabia, captured and looted the Abbasid city of Basra.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Fatimid general Jawhar al-Siqilli, who had fortified Ayn Shams just north of Cairo with a trench and wall, defeated the invading <b>Qarmatians</b> north of the city and forced them to withdraw from Egypt in disorder.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-24">24 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Qarmatians</b> of Bahrayn, an Isma'ili Shia militant movement centred in al-Ahsa in Eastern Arabia, sacked the Muslim holy city of Mecca amid the rituals of the Hajj.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-11">11 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Abu Tahir the <b>Qarmatians</b> raided Basra and Kufa, attacked pilgrim caravans and in 930 sacked Mecca, carrying the Black Stone to al-Ahsa until 951.</blockquote><a href="/state/qarmatians-of-bahrain">Qarmatians of Bahrain</a></li><li><blockquote>Yusuf was killed in 928 fighting the <b>Qarmatians</b> in Iraq, and the emirate collapsed in 929 amid struggles among his commanders.</blockquote><a href="/state/sajid-emirate">Sajid Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
